-- §18.2.2 (do-item sequences: an expression item is a MONADIC expression
-- item, `expr : m a`, and the last reached do-item provides the block
-- result), §18.8.6 (result-producing item: "Expression item `final` where
-- `final : m A`"), §18.3 (Lift_m applies only to items containing `!`
-- splices or mutable-variable current-value reads; a bare pure expression
-- contains neither).  Nothing in §18 licenses implicitly `pure`-lifting a
-- pure-typed final expression item, so this program -- whose do block over
-- `UIO Int` ends in the bare `Int` variable `n` -- must be rejected with a
-- type mismatch (n : Integer vs IO Void Int).
--
-- The compiler instead accepts it (an IO-family-only "corpus
-- accommodation": an inferable statement may check at the bare pure type).
-- The same shape over Option IS rejected (see sibling test
-- do-tail-missing-pure-option-mismatch.kp), so acceptance here is both
-- contrary to §18.8.6 and inconsistent across monads: beginners learn
-- "a do block may end in a plain value" from IO and are then baffled
-- when Option/user monads reject exactly that with a 1-3 error cascade.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 1
--! assertDiagnosticFamily error kappa.type.mismatch
module main

n : Int
let n = 42

main : UIO Int
let main = do
    printInt 1
    n
-- STATUS(a0497523): FAIL — accepted with 0 errors: the IO-family do block silently passes the bare pure Int tail through (Check.hs statementInferable corpus accommodation), contra §18.2.2/§18.8.6; the identical shape over Option is rejected
